# aarch64 (Apple Silicon laptop) direct dependencies for the ROS 2 SDK container
# image — the SAME set as requirements.in, which the `-r` below pulls in whole, with
# one substitution: torch/torchvision come from PyTorch's CPU index. This is an
# override layer, not a fork; edit shared pins in requirements.in.
#
# Why a second file has to exist. `torch==2.14.0` on PyPI is a DIFFERENT BUILD per
# architecture, and only one of the two runs on the machine that gets it:
#
#   x86_64  -> CUDA build. Correct for the deployed target. The EC2 instance is a
#              g4dn (T4) and it launches the detector with coco_device:=cuda, so
#              the CUDA half is the point (deployment/lib/go2-ec2-stack.ts).
#   aarch64 -> NVIDIA Grace/sbsa CUDA build. Bundles ~3.3 GB of nvidia-*-cu13
#              wheels and an NVPL-linked libtorch_cpu.so that SEGFAULTS at dlopen
#              on Apple Silicon under Docker Desktop. `import torch` dies with
#              SIGSEGV, which takes coco_detector_node with it (exit -11 at
#              startup, before it logs anything), so nothing publishes
#              /detected_objects or /annotated_image: no boxes in Lichtblick, no
#              'find' / 'follow me', nothing for the KVS producer to stream.
#
# So this is not a convenience build for laptops. On aarch64 the PyPI pin does not
# import at all, and the failure is silent in the image's own logs. The +cpu wheels
# are the same upstream release with the CUDA half omitted; they also take the
# closure from 15 nvidia-* packages to zero.
#
# `make lock` compiles this into requirements-arm64.txt, which docker/Dockerfile
# installs when `uname -m` is aarch64 (requirements.txt otherwise). Both closures
# are hashed, and `make audit` scans both.
-r requirements.in

# Emitted into the compiled file (uv --emit-index-url) so `pip install -r` can find
# the +cpu wheels without the Dockerfile having to repeat this URL.
--extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu

# The +cpu LOCAL VERSION, not a bare ==2.14.0. PEP 440 ignores the local segment
# when matching, so a bare pin is satisfied by the CUDA wheel as well — that is
# precisely the resolution that segfaults, and it also means an in-place
# `pip install torch==2.14.0` over a CUDA install is a silent no-op.
torch==2.14.0+cpu
torchvision==0.29.0+cpu
